# === Group 3: one dead branch ===============================================
#
# internal/ui/bordered/bordered.go:232 — the "ensure at least one line" fallback
# in buildContentLines. The loop above it iterates strings.Split(content, "\n"),
# and strings.Split ALWAYS returns at least one element: for "" it returns [""],
# for "\n" it returns ["", ""]. So `len(result) == 0` is unreachable and the
# fallback is inert.
#
# Verified rather than assumed, and pinned as executable proof by
# TestRender_TheEnsureAtLeastOneLineFallbackIsUnreachable, which states both the
# premise (Split never yields zero elements) and the consequence (every input
# produces exactly split-lines + 2 box lines). Removing the fallback would change
# nothing observable.
internal/ui/bordered/bordered.go:232:ARITHMETIC_BASE

# internal/config/scanner.go:49 — the `Name < Name` tiebreak at the end of the sort
# in Scan. Unreachable, and the reason is the dedup that runs on the line before:
# two survivors can only share a Path if they came from the same directory, and
# FoundProject.Name is derived from the single .dbx.toml in that directory. Same
# directory, same file, same first-sorted connection name. So by the time the sort
# runs, equal Path always implies equal Name, and `Name < Name` is false for every
# input the program can produce.
#
# Pinned as an invariant rather than as a fixture by
# TestScan_DuplicatePathsCollapseSoTheNameTiebreakIsUnreachable, which asserts the
# converse directly: within one scan, no two survivors share a Path with different
# Names. A change that broke the invariant would fail there rather than silently
# start depending on a comparator that can never return true.
internal/config/scanner.go:49:CONDITIONALS_BOUNDARY
internal/config/scanner.go:49:CONDITIONALS_NEGATION
internal/config/config.go:23:CONDITIONALS_NEGATION

# === Group 4: TIMED OUT, unkillable by any test =============================
#
# FOUR files, every entry the same shape: a loop guard that is also the only thing
# advancing the index, whose negation leaves the loop never terminating.
#
#   internal/ui/components/editor/highlight.go   86 line-comment scan, 142 and 150/152
#                                                the word scan, 186 punctuation, 193 unknown
#   internal/app/txn.go                          386 the line-comment scan, 407 the blank loop
#   internal/config/keybindings_groups.go        39  the group scan
#   internal/ui/bordered/bordered.go             251 and 268 the ANSI-escape scan
#
# A test cannot kill an infinite loop. The only outcomes are a hang — which the
# harness abandons, leaving the mutant absent from report.json and therefore
# indistinguishable from a kill — or a failure, which requires the test to DETECT the
# hang first. tokenizeWithin and highlightWithin in highlight_test.go do that, with
# a budget chosen to fire before the harness's own, and they catch most of
# highlight.go's. They cannot catch all of them: each hanging test burns its full
# budget and the harness's budget for the whole package is smaller than the sum.
#
# The count is NOT deterministic — highlight.go reported 9 timeouts on one run and 7
# on the next with an identical suite, because it depends on how much of the budget
# each test consumes and on CPU contention with the other workers. So these are
# CEILINGS, not an exact set. scripts/check_mutate_nc.py fails if a file exceeds its
# ceiling, fails if a file with timeouts has no ceiling at all, and prints the set
# for information.
#
# Ceilings are set at roughly TWICE the highest count observed, because the count
# moves with machine load: bordered.go produced 3 on one run and 4 on the next, and
# a ceiling sitting on the observed maximum is a ceiling that will flake. At twice
# the maximum an ordinary slow run passes, and a genuine increase — a new hang, or
# a change that makes more of the suite reach a mutated path — is still caught.
#
# The right long-term answer is a property/fuzz test with a per-input timeout
# enforced by the testing framework itself, which this project does not use yet.
TIMEOUT_MAX internal/ui/components/editor/highlight.go 16 the tokenizer loop guards are the only thing advancing the index
# grid/table.go times out on THREE mutants, all of them loop index advances:
# the F-key page lookup (for i := 1; i <= 9; i++) and the blank padding at the
# bottom of the row block (for i := visibleCount; i < contentHeight; i++), plus
# the negation of that loop's bound. Removing the advance makes either loop spin
# forever with no test able to bound it, which is the same shape as every other
# entry in this file and the reason the coefficient is 20 rather than the
# default 3. The ceiling is set at twice the observed count so that a genuine
# regression above three is a red build rather than a flake.
TIMEOUT_MAX internal/ui/components/grid/table.go 6 the page-lookup and row-padding loops; their index advance is the only thing terminating them
TIMEOUT_MAX internal/app/txn.go 10 the SQL lexer line-comment and blank loops
TIMEOUT_MAX internal/config/keybindings_groups.go 6 the group scan in digitRange
TIMEOUT_MAX internal/ui/bordered/bordered.go 8 the ANSI-escape scan
